is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Access switch VLAN/bridge membership on switch1.

Mechanism: The two laptops are no longer in the same Layer‑2 broadcast domain, so the NetBIOS/LLMNR/mDNS broadcasts used for “seeing” each other and ad‑hoc file sharing do not traverse between them. While inter‑VLAN routing may still permit ICMP, the discovery and SMB browse traffic they rely on is blocked at L2.

Localization: On switch1, ports are split across two bridges: br_vlan10 and br_vlan20. The switch shows eth3 and eth4 (where laptop2 and laptop3 connect) in br_vlan10, while eth7 (where laptop0 connects) is in br_vlan20:
- switch1: brctl show lists eth3/eth4 under br_vlan10 and eth7 under br_vlan20.
Host configs confirm the split:
- laptop2 (eth0 on cd_sw1_fa3_1): 172.16.10.12/24
- laptop3 (eth0 on cd_sw1_fa4_1): 172.16.10.13/24
- laptop0 (eth0 on cd_sw1_fa7_1): 172.16.20.10/24
Ping from laptop0 to laptop2 works with TTL=63 (via a router), indicating they are not on the same L2 segment.

Root cause: switch1 port eth7 (desk of one user) was reconfigured into VLAN20, separating it from the neighbor’s port(s) in VLAN10.

Corrective fix: Move switch1 port eth7 (cd_sw1_fa7_1) from br_vlan20 to br_vlan10 so both laptops share VLAN10 (172.16.10.0/24). After changing the port’s access VLAN, verify both hosts obtain/retain 172.16.10.x addresses and can discover each other. Alternatively, if VLAN separation is intentional, implement proper name resolution and file‑sharing across subnets (WINS/DNS/mDNS repeater) and firewall exceptions—but restoring eth7 to VLAN10 matches the users’ workflow. 
=== END ===